Number:    110982
Title:     Clustering for Approximate Similarity Search and Query Refinement
        in High-Dimensional Spaces

Based on these reviews, my recommendation as Associate Editor is that
you prepare a major revision of the paper along the lines suggested
by the reviewers and resubmit the
revised version for a second round of reviews.

Please pay particular attention to the following:

- reorganize the related work section and include missing references,
as suggested by rev #1. A more convincing discussion contrasting your
approach to other recent techiniques for approximate similarity
search is also needed.
As to this point, as a personal advice I suggest you to consider
recent work by Bennett, Fayyad, and Geiger (KDD 1999), where an
approach similar to Clindex is presented.
Also clearly explain how the paper differs from your previous works,
as requested by rev. #3.

- be explicit about the limitations of your approach (e.g. static data sets)

- considering the objections raised by rev #1 concerning "query
refinement", drop section 3.6 and change the title of the paper (and
include the keyword "clustering")

- provide a better motivation for the CF algorithm, and provide a
more clear description of its actions. Consider that two reviewers
observed that the reader can get confused by the absence of a clear
distinction between what the algorithm actually does and what it
could do. How the algorithm can be practically used is also an issue,
thus clarify how values of parameters can be chosen.

- clarify the role of the specific (CF) clustering algorithm you use,
this is needed to clearly focus the paper. On the one hand it seems
that CF has a major role in determining Clindex performance, on the
other hand this appears to contradict the statement at page 7.

- the experimental section should be strenghtened. In particular:

* comparison with at least one index structure other than the R*-tree
and explicitly designed to deal with high-dimensional data, such as
the X-tree, the SR-tree, or the VA-file, is needed.

* provide additional performance metrics, such as elapsed time

* experiment with larger data sets


