DETAILED COMMENTS

A.  Public Comments (these will be made available to the author)

     [Reviewer 1] "Clustering for Approximate Similarity Search and Query
Refinement in High-Dimensional Spaces" is an interesting research paper which
combinesthe results of two areas of database research, multidimensional
indexingknowledge discovery. A clustering algorithm is used to improve the
quality of approximate similarity search. The paper is in generalwell-organized
and sound but parts of the paper clearly need improvement.The most important
weakness of the paper regards the way in which theexperimental evaluation is
performed. Although the blocks of the clusterbased indexing approaches are
allowed to be arbitrarily unbalanced, onlythe number of retrieved blocks is
compared. The number of accesses,however, is not an appropriate cost measure as
the retrieval of largerblocks is much more expensive than the retrieval of
smaller blocks.To show how bad the chosen measure is, let us consider an
extreme exampleof a fictive clustering method that fills all clusters with only
veryfew points. Let 99% of the points be noise which is grouped in a separate
"cluster". If we make additionally sure that the noise-cluster is accessed
first, we would also have the 99% recall with only one page access.I do not
believe that the impressive results of the experimental evaluationare due to
such effects. But it is absolutely necessary to exclude suchdoubts. Therefore,
it is necessary to report not only the average numberof points which are in a
cluster, but also parameters such as the variance, and the maximum and minimum
number of points. Additionally to theexperiments varying the number of I/O
operations, experiments reporting(or varying) the actual I/O-cost (plus,
eventually, CPU cost, e.g. measured as the number of distance computations) are
required. The I/O cost of an access of b bytes can be determined by the formula
     T = b*t_transfer + t_seek + t_latencywhere the parameters of the disk
drive must be looked up in the handbook.Often the following parameters are
used:    t_transfer = 0.25 sec/MByte   t_seek     = 5 msec     t_latency  = 5
msecThis formula would also allow to evaluate how much of the
recall-improvementis achievable by simple blocksize optimization. The result of
such anexperiment should be that the clustering approach yields a substantially
higher recall improvement than simply adapting the blocksize of an index.
Further comments:Title of the paper: remove "Query Refinement" from the title.
The corresponding section 3.6 is so weak that it is not justified tomention
this in the title. (cf. below)General limitations of the approach:The
proposed indexing schema is primarily designed for static data sets,i.e. it
does not consider changes in the data distribution due to updates.I am not
generally bothered by this limitation, but it should be clearlypointed out at
some prominent position such as the introduction and conclusion, where dynamic
databases could be marked as "future work".In index construction, it is not
usual to assume that the whole dataset fits into main memory. Is it possible to
run the CF algorithm when the dataset is not main memory resident? If not, this
is also a limitationwhich should be stated explicitly.Two paramenters are
needed to calibrate the index: Kapa and Theta.It is pointed out in the paper
that such a calibration is necessary toachieve high performance, and rules of
thumb are given to find goodstart value for an experimental calibration. This
is not very satisfyingfrom an indexing point of view, because nobody really
wants to performextensive experiments before constructing an index. Either, the
choice of parameters must be described more directly as a set of guidelines or
even better, in a mathematical model which allows directlyto determine the
parameters, or this limitation must be clearly stated.p. 3-6, Background:The
related-work section is really not well-organized and should becompletely
rewritten in a clearly structured way. Techniques whichare of general interest
should be introduced concisely, andtechniques which are more related to the
Clindex approach shouldbe introduced in detail. It is mentioned, for instance,
