     [Reviewer 2] I found the paper interesting since searching for relevant
information in large databases is a pressing problem. Clindex might help to
reduce retrieval costs. However, I have some concerns regarding maintenance
costs and the experiments.As stated in the paper, the time complexity of
building the index is o(d*M^2). If d is large, M is almost equal to N (no two
points are likely to fall into the same cell in high-dimensional spaces). Thus,
o(d*M^2) seems to be very expensive. Moreover, it is necessary to rebuild the
index from time to time. I have looked at the demo on your web-page in order
to get a feeling for how fast the retrieval is. The efficiency of the algorithm
was very impressive (less than 2 seconds). However, the quality of the
similarity search was very poor. With all the examples I tried, I got no
(visually) similar image. From my perspective, it seems as if the clusters
consist of random subsets of the entire data base. I can not understand, how
your method has reached a recall of almost 100% with very few IOs. All the
queries I tried returned irrelevant images. The only exception was if there were
multiple copies of an image in the database. Consequently, you should better
explain what you mean with recall in your experiments (you used cross-validation
but how exactly). The figures seem very impressive, but a recall of 100% means
that all returned images are relevant for the given query. Looking at your demo,
this is certainly not the case.

     [Reviewer 3] The proposed indexing technique is definitely static (i.e.
insertion and deletions of objects are not supported): This should be explicitly
stated; otherwise, you should give some hint on how Clindex could be extended to
deal with dynamic datasets.Figure 3 (CF algorithm), step 5.2(b): The else
statement is not necessary, since you have to choose to which cluster insert
cell C regardless of the cardinality of \Psi (if this is >1, possibly clusters
are merged).Page 10, 3rd paragraph: You discuss about the CC procedure, giving
some intuition on possible heuristics; however, it is not clear which heuristics
are actually implemented and which are not (this is especially needed for the
presented image dataset).Page 13, 2nd para: You state that "it might be good
for the outlier cluster to be relatively large". However, on page 22, you set
\theta=0 in order to avoid most of the points falling into the outlier cluster.
Explain these contrasting statements.Experiments on other datasets are an
immediate need: at least, present something on the 500,000 images dataset.I
believe that a graph showing elapsed times needed to solve the queries could
also show advantages of the Clindex approach.You state that sequential disk
reads are helpful to reduce query costs: To this end, I suggest you to compare
Clindex with X-tree and not with R*-tree (indeed, X-tree exploit sequential disk
reads in super-nodes to reduce query resolution time).Finally, I believe that a
comparison with the VA-file (see ref. [39]) could also improve quality of the
paper. The VA-file is an indexing technique that exploits vector space
quantization and uses a hashing method to index resulting cells. In my view,
this is similar to the Clindex approach, except that no clustering is applied.
Therefore, you can show the effectiveness of the CF algorithm by comparing
Clindex and VA-file results.

     [Reviewer 4] The paper describes a technique for combining clusteringwith
indexing. However, it does not justify the approach well.1. How does this
clustering compare to conventional   techniques employed in database research
such as    KL transforms of the TV-tree? After applying such   transforms, it
may be sufficient to just index the    data using X-trees or SR-trees without
any clusteirng?   The performance could be much better than yours if the
dimensionality is reduced after the KL-transform.    Experiments are needed to
justify clindex.2. The paper only presents and evaluates   the effectiveness
of clustering. It overlooks the issues   in indexing once the leaf-level
clusters are formed and assumes   the cluster directory fits in memory, which
is not acceptable   in the database community. The index-level clusters may not
fit   in memory for two reasons: (1) database size, and (2) large
dimensionality which leads to higher number of clusters for a given
blocksize/filesize. 3. Cluster size of 300 (section 4.4) may not be good for
all data.    If the dimensionality is of the order of thousands    (as in the
case of text documents, or some image databases),    clusters of 300 imply a
lot of IOs to retrieve the   cluster. The results from 48-dimensional data may
not    apply in other cases. Experimentation with different datasets    of
varying dimensionality and recommendations based on them    would be useful.
4. Instead of comparing with the R*-tree, a better approach would be to
compare against the SR-tree (or an X-tree-based SR-tree), where the   centroid
information could be used. The comaprisons seem unfair right now   due to lack
of centroid information in R*-trees. As mentioned in (1),   you may want to
cluster the data using KL-transforms.5. Another useful attribute of the
clustering schemes missed out is   the time for clustering (and any
precomputation of nearest clusters:   see remarks on page 14) for different
large disk-based datasets.   There are a few typos in the paper:   Page 20,
Second para, second line: "clustering" and "non-clustering" are
interchanged.

