1.  Please summarize how this manuscript advanced this field of research and
contributes something new to the literature.

     [Reviewer 1] One of the first approaches to exploit explicit use of
clusteringalgorithms to improve the quality or an index structure

     [Reviewer 2] Accurate similarity search is known to be difficult and
expensive. The paper explains the inherent dificulties of similarity search (i.e
of nearest neighbor search in high-dimensional spaces) and suggests not to
search for accurate answers but only for good answers, i.e. an approximative
result. The proposed method (Clindex) reduces search costs effectively without
loosing too much in quality (i.e. recall remains very high even though only a
small number of IOs are performed). However, it is somehow difficult to see, how
the method performs in real application in terms of maintenance costs (insertion
of new objects seems to be quite expensive since the mapping file needs to be
sorted; reclustering might be required from time to time if the database grows)

     [Reviewer 3] The paper presents a new indexing method (clindex) to
approximate similarity search in (high-D) vector spaces. The proposed method
first perform a clustering of DB objects, based on a discrete subdivision of the
vector space; then, objects in resulting clusters are sequentially written to
disk in order to exploit the superior read speed with respect to random IOs
during the retrieval. This is a hot topic and presented results are promising.

     [Reviewer 4] The paper presents a technique for similarity searching in
image databases. In most cases, the image feature data areclustered. The
authors propose a mechanism for clustering,and subsequently enabling efficient
and effective searching.


